as no pawn there. So indeed, no en passant possible. So b7-b5 is safe? If Black plays b7-b5, White pawn on b4 is now adjacent to Black pawn on b5. On White\'s next turn, White can capture en passant: White\'s b4 pawn captures Black\'s b5 pawn (as if Black pawn moved to b6), resulting in White pawn on b6 and Black pawn removed. So b7-b5 is a mistake—it loses a pawn for nothing.  \n\nThus, b7-b5 should be avoided.  \n\nBest options: Develop pieces, control center.  \n\nPromising:  \n- **N: b8-c6** – Develops knight, attacks d4 and e5 squares, supports future d7-d5. Good.  \n- **P: d7-d5** – Takes center immediately.  \n- **P: e7-e5** – Also good.  \n\nReason: In opening, knights before pawns is fine, but central pawn moves are also strong. Since White has advanced b4, maybe White is playing a flank opening. Black should seize the center.  \n\n**P: d7-d5** immediately challenges center, opens lines for bishop and queen.  \n\nBut we must check: After d7-d5, White could play e2-e4, challenging. That’s fine.  \n\nGiven the valid moves list, I think **N: b8-c6** is a flexible, solid choice. It prepares to support d5 or e5 next.  \n\nAlso note that after N: b8-c6, if White plays something like Bb2 or a3, Black can follow with e5.  \n\nGiven the above, I choose **N: b8-c6**.  \n\nAnswer: N: b8-c6'}
